This file describes bugs that are not yet fixed and features that are not
yet implemented. Rewritten 2026-10-17 after the v1.1.33 release from a
full read-through of lib/, cli/, daemon/, hub/, snmpd/, the Makefile, CI
and the man pages. Every item cites file:line as of 1.1.33 (ae2af46).
Confidence is "confirmed" (full code path read) unless marked
"plausible" (depends on an OS/library fact not verifiable in-repo).
Items from the previous TODO that are still open are carried over in
section 10; everything previously marked [FIXED] is gone from here and
lives in CHANGELOG.md.

Priority legend: HIGH = wrong or dangerous behaviour reachable in a
normal deployment; MED = incorrect result, data/resource leak or a
documented feature that does not work; LOW = hygiene, edge cases, dead
code, doc drift.

The bhyve/VM track is in TODO2.

=== 1. Blocker: the rootless (1.0.0) model is not actually reachable ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.34] cli/main.cpp refused to start unless euid==0 AND
  uid!=0 (setuid only). Gate removed, HOME from passwd, runtime-dir
  check made non-root tolerant, lite-CI smoke step added.

  [FIXED in 1.1.39] per-user runtime root created AND chowned to the
  operator by crated (every privops connection + new `ensure_user_root`
  verb); rootless jail directories under /var/run/crate/<uid>/
  (RuntimePathsPure::isCrateJailPath for jail_query/console/info/clean);
  network teardown + bridge/auto ipfw through privops
  (RunNet::teardownIface / ipfwExec); healthcheck via JailExec; top via
  query_jail_rctl; console argv split; per-user network_pool split
  (network_pool_user_bits); clear refusals for NAT mode and
  auto_create_bridge under rootless.

  [FIXED in 1.1.40] the exec blocker. New privops verb `exec_in_jail`
  (option A): crate(1) sends argv/user/TERM plus its stdin/stdout/
  stderr as nvlist descriptors; crated pdfork()s, dup2s, closefrom,
  jail_attach(jid), switches to the jail-side user, builds a minimal
  env and execve()s; the process descriptor comes back in the reply
  and JailExec waits on it with kqueue EVFILT_PROCDESC (no daemon
  thread pinned; the command dies with `crate run`). Authz is STRICT:
  the jid must be registered to the caller — no bootstrap concession
  (DenyUnownedJid). JailExec::execInJail/execInJailGetOutput take the
  privops branch whenever euid != 0 and a socket is detected, so
  `crate run`, `crate console`, `crate update --pkg-only`, `crate
  info`, healthchecks and the per-jail network setup all go through
  it unchanged. libnv transport only (HTTP answers 400).

  [FIXED in 1.1.41] Ctrl-C/SIGTERM under rootless: the in-jail
  command is crated's child, so a terminal signal never reached it and
  `crate run` sat in its kqueue wait forever. The SIGINT/SIGTERM
  handler now forwards the signal through the process descriptors
  being waited on (JailExec::forwardSignalToPrivopsChildren → pdkill),
  and the wait becomes bounded (5 s grace, then "killed by SIGTERM")
  so teardown runs even if the command ignores the signal. Also
  fixed: host-side static-mac (`set_iface_mac`), NAT instance delete
  (`delete_ipfw_nat`), auto_create_bridge (`create_bridge`, cloner
  names bridge<N> only), `crate info` RCTL limits (query_jail_rctl
  `limits: true` → rctl -h), crate(1) audit fallback to
  /var/run/crate/<uid>/crate-audit.log with a one-time warning instead
  of a silent drop, `crate doctor` without root (per-user root checked
  instead of the root-owned tree; crated.conf unreadable = expected),
  and — security — crated's per-user audit.log open (root, inside a
  directory the operator owns since 1.1.39) now O_NOFOLLOW + fstat
  (plain file, one link, owner root or the operator) and chowned to
  the operator so the "operator-readable copy" is readable.

  [FIXED in 1.1.42] GUI compositor + wayvnc start through
  JailExec::spawnInJail (root: fork+jail_attach; rootless: exec_in_jail
  with a process descriptor; signalSpawned/waitSpawned hide the
  difference) — the last raw `jexec` in crate(1). Daemon-started
  containers (control-socket / HTTP `start`+`restart`) are now recorded
  in the jid→owner registry: crated watches for the jail whose kernel
  name ends in `_pid<crate run pid>` (RunPure::jailXnameHasPid) and
  records the control-socket peer uid (0 = admin for the bearer-token
  HTTP path). The operator who started a container can exec/console
  into it; other tenants lose the bootstrap Allow on it.

* (HIGH) exec_in_jail follow-ups:
    - no on-hardware run yet: the whole rootless `crate run` path
      (ensure_user_root → create_jail → mounts → exec_in_jail …) has
      never been exercised end to end; docs/on-hardware-validation.md
      §4.1 is the first thing to run.
    - the daemon's child inherits crated's rlimits and cpuset; RCTL
      rules on the jail still apply (they key on the jail), but
      per-process limits should come from the jail, not the daemon.
    - the child calls getpwnam() after fork in a multi-threaded
      daemon (jemalloc atfork makes it safe in practice); resolving
      the user from <jailPath>/etc/master.passwd in the parent would
      remove the dependency.

* (HIGH) remaining direct privileged calls under rootless:
    - run.cpp:1595 host v6 epair address (NAT mode only).
    - NAT mode as a whole: Ctx::FwSlots/FwUsers in root-owned
      /var/run/crate (ctx.cpp:191, locs.cpp:11,14), ip.forwarding
      sysctls, setupFirewallRules' ipfw rules partially via privops.
      Now refused up front under rootless. Making NAT mode rootless
      needs the slot allocator in the daemon (an `allocate_fw_slot`/
      `release_fw_slot` pair keyed by peer uid+pid) and a sysctl verb.
    - lib/inter_dns.cpp:36-56 writes /etc/hosts and the unbound
      conf.d file directly (root-only by nature; document, or move the
      rebuild into crated as a verb).
    - stack.cpp:787-797 (bridge create), stack.cpp:294 (unbound on
      :53) — stack is root-only today.
    - lib/pfctl_ops.cpp:28 PfLock opens /var/run/crate/pfctl.lock 0600;
      non-root open fails and held() is never checked → no
      serialisation (moot while pf goes through privops, which
      serialises in the daemon).
    - `logs` is overridable from ~/.config/crate/crate.yml, so a user
      still chooses where their own audit trail goes (the daemon's
      audit.log is the record that counts for privops).
    - `create_bridge` only accepts cloner names (bridge<N>); a spec
      with auto_create_bridge and a renamed bridge (e.g. "lan0") gets a
      clear 400 under rootless — needs create + `name` rename in the
      daemon if that is wanted.

=== 2. crated privops / HTTP — security ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.35] the four HIGH items: create_jail `parameters`
  whitelist (PrivOpsPure::validateJailParameters); path-scoped verbs
  confined to the caller's prefix when path_master_prefix is set
  (Decision::DenyUnownedPath); realpath() canonicalisation of every
  path argument in privops_handlers.cpp + lstat/O_EXCL mount targets
  in run_gui.cpp; ws-console on checkBearerAuthFull. Also
  routes.cpp jsonError() escaping.

* (MED, residual of the 1.1.35 canonicalisation) the realpath() check
  and the nmount()/jail -c/devfs call are two steps; a symlink swapped
  in between still wins the race. Closing it needs fd-based operation:
  open(O_RESOLVE_BENEATH) relative to the caller's prefix and
  nmount with "fspath" replaced by an fd ("fd" iov) / jail path via
  fdescfs, or at least a second realpath() after the operation with
  rollback. Same for lib/run_gui.cpp's mount targets (lstat then
  mount). Low practical exposure (needs a compromised jail racing
  its own operator's `crate run`), but the only remaining gap in the
  path chain.

  [FIXED in 1.1.36] viewer token on GET /api/v1/containers, /gui,
  /host; pool gate on GET /exports/:filename
  (TransferPure::artifactContainerName); pf anchor verbs name-scoped
  (Decision::DenyForeignAnchor); attach_zfs/detach_zfs gate the jid;
  jid→owner registry reconciled against live jails at startup and on
  every lookup hit; destroy_jail forgets by pre-resolved jid.

  [FIXED in 1.1.43] interface-named verbs gated: iface→owner registry
  (lib/iface_owner_registry{,_pure}.*, /var/db/crate/iface_owners.tsv)
  fed by create_epair / teardown_iface, reconciled against the host's
  `a` halves; checkOwnedIface on configure_iface's ifname,
  teardown_iface, bridge_*_member's member, reclaim's iface,
  set_iface_up/disable_iface_offload/set_iface_inet_addr/set_iface_mac.
  Non-epair names → DenyHostIface for per-user callers; unknown epair →
  bootstrap Allow. Also: validateConfigureIface rejected the move-only
  request run_net.cpp sends for EVERY rootless move (400) — fixed;
  nvlist header peeked before nvlist_recv (1 MiB / 3 descriptors);
  HTTP DELETE /containers/:name goes through stopCrate (no more
  kill -9 + jail -r leaking mounts/epairs/anchors) and reports
  was_running; crated.conf role values validated, top-level vs
  rootless.* override warned.

* (by design, documented) a pool-scoped ADMIN token can POST
  /imports/:name + /containers/:name/start for a name in its pool and
  have crated run that .crate as root (the spec may declare host
  shares/scripts). trust-model §2b already states admin tokens are
  host-wide, not pool-confined; only viewer/pool-scoped tokens carry
  isolation. Making this real needs the spawned `crate run` to drop to
  the token's mapped uid — part of the rootless §1 work, not a gate.

  [FIXED in 1.1.37] pf rules over a pipe (`pfctl -f -`,
  Util::execCommandWithInput); SO_RCVTIMEO/SO_SNDTIMEO + 64-connection
  cap on privops/control-socket listeners, handshake timeout + session
  cap on ws-console; WsPure::kMaxFramePayload 1 MiB;
  listen.max_body_mb / listen.http_workers for httplib;
  accept4(SOCK_CLOEXEC) + FD_CLOEXEC on the httplib listener;
  control-socket restart = bounded stop + detached spawnCrateRun;
  configure_iface shape check before moveToVnet.

* (MED) Synchronous HTTP handlers remain synchronous — by choice in
  1.1.37 (the worker pool now absorbs them): routes.cpp stop (≤ 11 s),
  restart (stop phase ≤ 11 s, then spawn), export (`tar | xz --extreme`
  inline, minutes). `crate migrate` reads the export response (file,
  size, sha256) and then downloads, so making export asynchronous
  (202 + poll) needs a migrate client change in the same release;
  stop/restart could return 202 after SIGTERM with a detached
  wait/KILL child, but that changes the documented `stopped:true`
  contract. Decide API shape first, then do both ends.

* (LOW) Registry write happens after `jail -c` returns (:663-671);
  until then the new jid/path are unowned (bootstrap Allow).
* (LOW) crated.8:135-136 says DELETE /containers/:name removes the
  dataset; it stops the container (via stopCrate since 1.1.43) and
  nothing else — fix the man page or implement the dataset removal.
* (LOW) privops_socket_mode gets no tightness warning unlike
  listen.unix_mode.
* (LOW) handlers at privops_handlers.cpp :171,560,577 return raw
  strerror/e.what() that may echo host paths. (jsonError escaping
  fixed in 1.1.35.)

=== 3. Networking runtime ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.38] bridge/auto pf anchor flushed at teardown; `crate
  clean` sweeps stale crate/* anchors and orphaned v4/v6 pool leases;
  rdr host-port collision warning; `firewall:` policy applied in bridge
  mode (static/pool IP); static-mac hashed from the jail name; ipfw ids
  interleaved (40000+2*jid / 40001+2*jid, jid <= 9999, throttle jid <=
  4999); net.inet6.ip6.forwarding restored. Still open: v4 forwarding is
  restored only when allowOutbound() (run_net.cpp:498, inbound-only
  jails leave it at 1).

  [FIXED in 1.1.39] two users on one host allocated the same
  network_pool IP: per-uid sub-block via PerUserNetPure::composeIpv4
  (network_pool_user_bits, default 8; v6: network_pool6_user_bits 16),
  addresses keep the pool prefix/gateway.

  [FIXED in 1.1.44] MAC portacl rules merged into the host-wide list on
  start and removed on stop (MacPure::merge/removePortaclRules,
  MacOps::add/removePortaclRules, RunAtEnd); `crate stats` parses the
  netstat --libxo JSON (LifecyclePure::parseNetstatIfaceBytesJson);
  IPv6 epair pairs one /126 each with two-hextet overflow
  (RunPure::epairIp6); options/net ip / gateway / ip6 / extra[]
  interface validated at spec parse (SpecPure::validateStaticIp4/6,
  validateGateway4; PrivOpsPure::validateIfaceName); run_net.cpp
  prefix-length guards (v4 ≤ 32, v6 numeric ≤ 128); epair unit parsed
  defensively (RunPure::epairUnit) instead of substr(); net.cpp
  netmask vector size check; ugidfw add argv split per word;
  healthcheck interval 0 rejected.

* (LOW) ipfw_ops.cpp:85 treats EINVAL from IP_FW_XGET as "native API
  available" — EINVAL can also mean an unsupported opcode on an older
  kernel; needs a FreeBSD check before changing.
* (LOW) mac_ops.cpp removeUgidfwRules deletes by list-line index (a
  concurrent add shifts indexes); the native path should match rules
  by content.
* (LOW) healthcheck `retries: 0` means the first failure is UNHEALTHY —
  that is the documented meaning of zero retries; left as is.

=== 4. crate stack ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.45] pool IPs no longer allocated/advertised for
  prebuilt `crate:` entries (their spec owns their network; a WARN
  says they are outside stack DNS/hosts); network_policy rule block
  picked from the live `ipfw list` (StackPure::freePolicySlot) instead
  of a per-process counter, cleanup RunAtEnd armed before the first
  addRule, otherStackSubnets = the stack's other networks,
  net.link.bridge.ipfw=1 set when a policy is applied, `stack down`
  deletes the bridge's policy rules (policyRulesForBridge); `stack
  exec`/`status` match jails exactly (jailNameMatchesContainer — the
  substring match ran `stack exec db` in jail "mydb_pid…"); mergeSpecs
  carries diskQuota, securelevel, childrenMax, cpuset, cronJobs,
  depends and an explicit services/auto_start: false
  (servicesAutoStartExplicit); subnet validated as CIDR at parse;
  unbound exit status decoded with WIFEXITED/WIFSIGNALED; `stack down`
  actually stops the containers (stopCrate per matching jail);
  validateStackName cap = NameLimits::kJailName.

* (MED) allocateIpPool is in-process only — not persisted, not
  reconciled with network_lease; two `stack up` runs of different
  stacks on one subnet can hand out the same address. Needs the lease
  file (lib/ip_alloc) or a stack-level lease under /var/run/crate.
* (LOW) no unit test round-trips every Spec field through mergeSpecs:
  lib/spec.cpp is not in TEST_LINK_SRCS (yaml-cpp), so the test needs
  either a pure "field list" or a FreeBSD functional test.
* (LOW) network_policy: a rule whose `proto` is omitted produces an
  ipfw syntax error at addRule (now cleaned up, but still fails the
  stack); validate rule.proto at parse.

=== 5. Spec parsing, config, registries ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.46] options map form rejects unknown option names;
  security/securelevel and enforce_statfs accept the documented -1
  (SpecPure::parseIntInRange); spec-registry name rule = jail name
  rule (128 chars, '.' allowed); spec registry + network_lease(6) lock
  a stable `<path>.lock` file instead of the renamed-over inode;
  crate.yml applied all-or-nothing per file with unknown keys
  reported; crate.yml securelevel / children_max wired as defaults
  for specs at -1 (both now default to unset, so behaviour is
  unchanged until configured); fileExists/dirExists use S_ISREG/
  S_ISDIR; ports validated 1..65535 and ranges non-reversed
  (parsePort/parsePortRange) for net inbound, firewall allow-tcp/udp,
  mac_portacl/allow_ports, gui/vnc_port; restart/delay 0 rejected;
  `restart: always` no longer loops after Ctrl-C / crate stop (exit
  128+SIGINT/SIGTERM breaks the loop) and an unknown policy in an old
  .crate is reported and treated as "no"; vlan parsed as a bounded
  spec value (1..4094) instead of as<int>() → "internal error";
  files/ rejects unknown sub-keys and a non-list share; `x11: maybe`
  rejected; cron schedule/command single-line with 5 fields or
  @keyword; audit file write loops to completion; $HOME alone is
  substituted and $HOMER is not; validate_pure recommends
  gui/mode=headless (x11/mode has no headless) and drops the dead
  gui/mode == "shared" branch.

* (LOW) firewall/allow-tcp|udp with a map value and mac_portacl
  non-map / allow_ports non-sequence are still silently ignored
  (spec.cpp firewall / security_advanced blocks) — mirror the
  files/ handling.
* (LOW) Duplicate top-level keys: only `scripts` is checked
  (spec.cpp); other sections are processed twice (lists append) or
  last-wins. (plausible — yaml-cpp iteration semantics)
* (LOW) locs.cpp:18-22 static initialiser calls getSysctlString; a
  throw there is std::terminate before main.
* (LOW) lib/doctor.cpp: :77 WARNs when ipfw is missing on pf-only
  hosts; `pf` not in kModules; :204/:317 hardcode /var/log/crate
  ignoring Config.logs; :327 threshold is 100 MB while the
  comment/section say 50.
* (LOW) `unless-stopped` == `always` in cli/main.cpp; no backoff
  between restarts.

=== 6. crate doctor ===

* (HIGH) Wrong absolute paths → false FAIL on every FreeBSD host:
  lib/doctor.cpp:129 requires /usr/bin/jls (real /usr/sbin/jls,
  pathnames.h:96) and :134/:425 use /sbin/rctl (real /usr/bin/rctl,
  pathnames.h:75). `crate doctor` always reports a required-command
  FAIL (exit 2) and always skips the RCTL presence check. Use the
  CRATE_PATH_* constants.
* (HIGH) doctor.cpp:73 probes racct with `kldstat -n racct`; RACCT is
  a kernel option, not a module, so this is WARN on every host and
  the exit code is permanently >= 1. Check the sysctl
  kern.racct.enable instead. (plausible — FreeBSD fact)

=== 7. Lifecycle, storage, GUI, monitoring, DNS, VPN ===

* (HIGH) `crate snapshot restore` NULL-derefs libzfs:
  lib/zfs_ops.cpp:84 calls zfs_rollback(zhp, nullptr, B_FALSE) with
  the SNAPSHOT handle as the first argument; the signature is
  zfs_rollback(zfs_handle_t *fs, zfs_handle_t *snap, boolean_t force)
  and libzfs dereferences `snap` unconditionally → SIGSEGV before the
  exec fallback can run. Requires HAVE_LIBZFS, which port/Makefile:50
  sets for the FreeBSD port. Open the parent dataset for `fs`, the
  snapshot for `snap`. Same shape at :349: zfs_show_diffs(zhp, outFd,
  fromsnap, tosnap, flags) is passed snap2 as fromsnap and nullptr as
  tosnap, so with two snapshots it shows snap2 → live filesystem, not
  snap1 → snap2 (plausible — libzfs API semantics).

* (HIGH) `crate export` archives everything mounted inside the jail
  root: lib/export.cpp:103 `tar cf - -C <jailPath> .` with no
  --one-file-system / exclusions. `crate run` nullfs-mounts host
  dirs/share, files/share, X11/Wayland/Pulse sockets and devfs under
  J(...) (run.cpp:752,1757,1791,1802; run_gui.cpp:316,739,828). A spec
  sharing $HOME exports the user's host directory into a shareable
  .crate (data leak + bloat + /dev nodes packed). create.cpp unmounts
  before tar (:541-543); export has no equivalent. README:18 and
  crate.5:82 carry no caveat. Fix: --one-file-system plus explicit
  --exclude of every share destination.

* (HIGH) `crate inter-dns` never emits any crate jail (feature dead):
  the kernel jail name is <name>_pid<pid> (run.cpp:907, run_jail.cpp
  :41-104); inter_dns.cpp:78 drops any name failing
  InterDnsPure::validateHostname, and inter_dns_pure.cpp:22-27 rejects
  `_`. collectEntries() is always empty → /etc/hosts gets an empty
  marker block and crate.conf only the local-zone. Strip the `_pid`
  suffix (nameComponent) before validation. Once fixed, multi-IP jails
  still break the output: jail_query.cpp:115,195 store ip4.addr via
  jailparam_export, which comma-joins arrays, so inter_dns_pure.cpp
  :64,78 emit `10.0.0.1,10.0.0.2` into hosts/local-data and unbound
  rejects the file.

  [FIXED in 1.1.39] `crate console`: "login -f <user>" was one argv
  element to jexec; split into words, extra args appended verbatim.
  Still root-only (jexec) until the exec verb from §1 exists.

* (MED) Auto-incremental backup/replicate chain is corrupt after any
  failure or mixed use: backup.cpp:68 takes the snapshot BEFORE
  `zfs send`; on send failure the @backup-<ts> snapshot persists and
  pickLatestBackupSuffix (zfs_dataset.cpp:27-39) later selects it as
  the incremental base although no stream holds it → unrestorable
  .inc-from-X file / remote "incremental source does not match".
  `crate backup` and `crate replicate` share the backup- namespace,
  so a local backup silently becomes the base of the next remote
  increment. BackupPure::buildDestroySnapshotArgv (backup_pure.cpp
  :130) has no caller — nothing ever destroys backup snapshots. `zfs
  list -r` (zfs_dataset.cpp:31) includes descendants and the regex
  (zfs_dataset_pure.cpp:18) does not pin the dataset. warm.cpp:48-56
  likewise leaves the snapshot behind if `zfs clone` fails.

* (MED) backup-prune mis-classifies chained incrementals as orphans
  and `--delete-orphans` deletes them: backup_prune_pure.cpp:262-286
  builds keptSuffixes only from kept FULLS, but --auto-incremental
  bases each run on the latest backup-* snapshot, i.e. the previous
  incremental; inc2 (inc-from-<inc1>) is always "orphan" even when F
  and inc1 are kept. tests/unit/backup_prune_pure_test.cpp:295-343
  only covers incrementals based on a full.

* (MED) `crate update --pkg-only` cannot work on crates built with
  `pkg:`: create.cpp:188 runs `pkg delete -f pkg`, :347-350 removes
  /var/db/pkg, and rmdirHierExcept(/usr/sbin) drops /usr/sbin/pkg;
  update.cpp:72 then jexec's /usr/sbin/pkg upgrade → ENOENT (or, after
  a bootstrap, an empty package DB). CHANGELOG 0.8.41 presents this as
  the supported upgrade path. Either keep pkg + its DB when the spec
  installs packages, or make update bootstrap pkg and re-register.

* (MED) ZfsOps::send/recv exec fallback silently succeeds on fork()
  failure (zfs_ops.cpp:385-399, 413-427): pid == -1 matches neither
  branch; restoreCrate then prints "recovered" with nothing received;
  waitpid return unchecked. lzc_receive(targetDataset) (:402-411)
  needs a fs@snap name; `--to pool/jails/name` always yields EINVAL,
  so the "native libzfs" label printed at backup.cpp:110 is never
  true (plausible).

* (MED) `crate restart` drops the original run's CLI options:
  lifecycle.cpp:512-518 forwards only runCrateFile and logProgress;
  --name / --warm-base / --var are lost, so the container comes back
  under a different name/config. stopCrate also returns true after
  destroy_jail soft-fails (:431-452), so restart proceeds while the old
  jail still exists; `crate stop` exits 0 with "stopped" in that case.

* (MED) `crate clean` races an in-progress `crate run`:
  clean.cpp:50-78 rmdirHier's every jail-* directory not in
  JailQuery::getAllJails(); run.cpp:317 creates and populates
  jail-<name>-<hex> BEFORE jail_set, so a concurrent clean deletes a
  container mid-extraction (plausible — timing). chroot-create-* dirs
  (create.cpp:441) are never swept.

* (MED) Predictable world-writable /tmp scratch files (same class as
  the 1.1.25 gui.cpp fix): run_gui.cpp:201 /tmp/crate-vncpw-<N>, :449
  /tmp/crate-xorg-<N>.conf, :911 /tmp/crate-clipboard-<name>.sh;
  display N is global (gui_registry.cpp:60 starts at 10).
  Util::Fs::writeFile is O_NOFOLLOW|O_TRUNC so symlinks are blocked,
  but a pre-created attacker-owned 0666 regular file is truncated and
  written: the VNC password lands in the attacker's file; the Xorg
  conf has no chmod so ModulePath/Load can be rewritten between write
  and execl (:462); a FIFO blocks open() forever. Use mkstemp /
  mkdtemp as gui.cpp now does.

* (MED) Unauthenticated VNC bound to all interfaces by default:
  run_gui.cpp:189-195 runs x11vnc `-shared -forever -nopw` with no
  -localhost; vnc_server.cpp:141 never sets listenInterface
  (libvncserver default INADDR_ANY). crate.5:270-277 documents no
  exposure caveat, while the compositor path (:1087-1100) defaults to
  127.0.0.1 with a warning. Default to loopback (reuse gui.vnc_bind).

* (MED) Migrate token IS visible in `ps`: migrate.cpp:73-74 puts
  `Authorization: Bearer <token>` in curl's argv (the comment admits
  it); README.md:144 claims it never appears on the command line.
  Use `-H @file` / --config. migrate_pure.cpp:146 also accepts explicit
  http:// (plaintext token), and the artifact is uploaded without
  checking the sha256 the export response carries (migrate.cpp:81,164).

* (MED) `crate top` DISK column is write bandwidth, not usage:
  top_pure.cpp:148 maps `writebps` to diskBytes, rendered as "DISK
  352.5 MB" (README.md:274-278). cputime from rctl is whole seconds,
  so at 1 Hz cpuPercent (top_pure.cpp:64-69) quantises to 0/100/200%.

* (LOW) lifecycle.cpp:272 logsTarget unvalidated →
  `crate logs ../../x` reads /var/log/x/console.log (unprivileged
  read; minor); JSON at :180-189 emits jail->name / rctl values
  unescaped (non-crate jails can have arbitrary names). Follow mode
  never notices rotation/truncation.
* (LOW) throttle.cpp:98-141 `--clear --show` clears, then errors
  "at least one of --ingress..."; retune.cpp:41-42 `--clear KEY`
  alone is rejected; retune_pure.cpp:35 lists `cpuset`, not an RCTL
